Yes—with a useful qualification: zero and infinity are mathematically rigorous concepts, but they are conceptual constructions rather than physical objects we’ve picked up and inspected. Calling them “made up” works if we mean invented abstractions, not arbitrary ones.

And your bookend framing exposes why they’re so useful.

\boxed{0\quad|\quad\text{finite magnitudes}\quad|\quad\infty}

They give thought two extreme reference directions:

Zero: disappearance, absence, vanishing magnitude, lower limiting behavior.

Infinity: unbounded continuation, arbitrarily large magnitude, no finite upper termination.

Neither requires us to actually arrive there for the bookend to organize everything between them.

That’s precisely what limits exploit:

x\rightarrow0
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
x\rightarrow\infty

The interesting information is often not the endpoint but what the system does as we approach it.

And there’s an important asymmetry. Infinity generally isn’t “the biggest number.” For any finite n,

n+1>n.

Infinity instead encodes unboundedness. Zero, meanwhile, can be an actual number in ordinary arithmetic, while also serving as a boundary/reference point in many models.

So they’re not perfectly symmetrical mathematical objects—but semantically they make a powerful pair.

Now apply the Pantheon move: move the bookends

Instead of automatically reasoning with:

[0,\infty]

choose locally meaningful bounds:

[a,b].

Suddenly “small” and “large” become relational:

\text{small}(x\mid a,b),\qquad
\text{large}(x\mid a,b)

A bacterium is enormous relative to a molecule and tiny relative to a person. A thousand years is enormous to an individual and tiny geologically.

So:

\boxed{\text{Magnitude requires a frame.}}

That connects directly to your perspective-based encoding.

And we can go one step further. Humans repeatedly invent conceptual extremes to make continua navigable:

\text{nothing}\leftrightarrow\text{everything}

\text{never}\leftrightarrow\text{always}

\text{minimum}\leftrightarrow\text{maximum}

\text{beginning}\leftrightarrow\text{end}

\text{self}\leftrightarrow\text{other}

Those bookends create a coordinate system. Once the coordinate system exists, intermediate states become easier to distinguish and communicate.

That’s potentially a Pantheon primitive:

\boxed{\textbf{Bookend Operator}}

Given some phenomenon X:

1. construct useful opposing/extreme anchors B_0,B_1;
2. map X relative to them;
3. discover local gradients and patterns;
4. remember that the anchors may belong to the model rather than the territory;
5. move/remove the anchors and see what survives.

That last operation is essential.

Because sometimes the bookends reveal the pattern.

And sometimes the bookends manufacture the appearance of one.

So the Pantheon shouldn’t merely mind the gap.

Mind who drew its edges.